What:           /sys/devices/.../ssc_time_reference
Date:           November 2026
Contact:        Uke Linux maintainers
Description:
                Root-readable diagnostic (0400) on the native Uke SSC
                controller. One demand read snapshots the current generation
                and existing admitted sensor-cache tick/sequence metadata
                under the controller state lock. On ARM64 it brackets the
                Linux architectural counter read with CLOCK_BOOTTIME reads
                and reports CNTFRQ. It does not request firmware data, start
                a sensor, alter a buffer/rate, poll in the background or
                publish a sample.

                The first line has schema=1 and admitted=0, generation,
                connected, reference_available, cntfrq_hz,
                linux_arch_counter, boottime_before_ns, boottime_after_ns
                and sensor_count. A reference is available only for a current
                controller with a nonzero counter/frequency and ordered host
                bounds. The counter is the kernel's architectural counter;
                this does not assert a virtual/physical counter selection,
                a fitted SSC counter domain, or a shared epoch/rate.

                One line per declared driver index reports sensor,
                cached_valid, stream_active, sequence and sensor_ticks.
                Invalid/non-current cache fields are zeroed. A valid cache
                may be old or inactive, including an on-change state; neither
                reading nor the current host bracket makes it fresh. No
                physical sample value, SUID, client ID or calibration data
                is exposed. The index follows this driver's existing
                descriptor array, rather than establishing a fitted sensor.

                This is evidence for separate clock-domain qualification.
                admitted remains zero. Consumers must not infer acquisition
                time, a mount transform, frame validity or production Motion1
                admission from this diagnostic or a kernel version.
